y within the boundaries of the  nation  when you look for example at the tragedy  that followed so many other sanctions  around iraq  in the uh 90s you see a period in which  the people of iraq suffered more than  their leadership  despite attempts certainly by the united  nations to make sure that they were  targeting  the leadership rather than the people  and in doing so what we saw was actually  that sanctions  almost invariably failed they made those  who were sanctioning them be seen  as the enemy not in any way as a partner  in the overthrow of a dictatorship but  now we're seeing something change  we're seeing a different way in which  sanctions are being applied  in fact going back to the idea of single  combat  between leaders this is the first time  that war has been  personalized perhaps for several hundred  years because what we're seeing with  sanctions  latest of which are being called  magnitsky sanctions after of course bill  browder's  partner in russian business who was  murdered by the putin regime  what we're seeing is sanctions targeted  on individuals we're seeing  named people connected to human rights  violations connected to  abuses of power having their interests  denied  the intention of course is pretty simple  that they won't be able to enjoy the  fruits of their ill-gotten gains they  won't be able to  shop at harrods they won't be able to  walk in st james's park  they won't be able to take in the  champs-elysees if  they are sanctioned directly and  personally  but are they really working well  we're going to find out and the reason  we're going to find out is because  they're being rolled out  even more frequently than they ever have  before we're seeing the numbers of  sanctioned individuals  rising only the other day well  okay a few months ago we heard carrie  lam the chief executive of hong kong  saying that she didn't even have a bank  account  and she had to be paid in cash because  of u.s sanctions  and after dominic raab's a