e that's an inescapable side of  human nature  you know it's much easier it's much more  natural to respond  to horrors than it is to enter into a  quite technical discussion  of the costs and benefits of particular  sanctions  but let's ask the question what can a  country  practically do if it is  disapproving of policy in another  country  or indeed if it concludes that crimes  against humanity  in extremists are taking place right  it's it's a question we should ask more  often  you can imagine a spectrum at one end of  the spectrum  you could say there is an absolute  moral obligation always and everywhere  to intervene against genocide whatever  the cost  so we will get together an international  coalition if we can and we will send  troops into  sin kyan or arakan or whatever the  disputed area is  uh we will seek to overthrow the  venezuelan government or be defeated in  the attempt right that would be  the one extreme of the spectrum the  other extreme of the spectrum which say  you know what  there's really not much we can do we  will support a  condemnatory resolution in the u.n we  might uh  tick off an ambassador we might welcome  some refugees but there's not  really anything practically we can do  now the question that we need to face  that every democracy should be asking  itself if it wants to be  interested and engaged in international  affairs  is where is the optimum point on that  spectrum  where is the point on that line where  in a practical and achievable way  you are most likely to affect policy  change  in another country and here is  one thing that i can say with absolute  certainty generalized trade sanctions  are not the answer they are not on that  spectrum  at all they're not just useless they do  more harm than good they hurt  all the wrong people they hurt ordinary  folk in the other country and in your  own country  and if anything they prop up the regime  of which you disapprove  and drive support for the dictators and  the autocra