ching to payments in our national  currencies and global currencies that  serve as an alternative to the dollar  we need to move away from using  international payment systems controlled  by the west so  by enforcing sanctions so zealously  which is obviously tempting and there  can be very good reasons to do that  you're in danger of creating a war on  drugs problem which is that you make the  black market so huge that it becomes  almost bigger than the illicit  market and you create um entire  economies outside  the rule of law dan hannon peter keatley  uh  asked a question he's he says even if  sanctions are not  very effective and we've gone through  some of the reasons why they might not  be tonight  either the generalized ones or the more  specific targeted ones  which you remain more fashionable he  says even they're not very effective  are they not better than nothing no  they're worse than nothing  they they are not even the targeted ones  um  some very specific diplomatic measures  against individuals may be very useful  and uh and if they're very micro  targeted they can be useful yes um  but the the the the kinds of sanctions  that we're talking about say in  venezuela  i think are a good example of how you  actually  exacerbate the problem and i'd like if i  may just to come back for a second to  that question  um not least because both of the the the  questioners  are very distinguished people i know  them both and it's great that you have  quality as well as quantity in this uh  in this  audience as the spectator dan i don't  think  where else so but but venezuela i think  is a very  neat case in point first of all they  the the sanctions were already being  used pretty much  uh globally by chavista uh fellow  travelers  and enthusiasts before they'd been  applied  so when the when the venus venezuelan  economy began to collapse once the spike  in oil  was passed the the go-to excuse for  every marxist sympathizer in every  british university in the left bank in  all over the world was oh this is a kind  of you know uh  cia uh hit job you know sabotage  sanctions all the  at that stage there were almost no  i think there may have been some  restrictions on buying venezuelan  government debt but that was it right  and  again it's a slightly odd thing if you  you know if i were  if i were a cia operative and my job  were to  sabotage the venezuelan economy what  would i do i mean you know i turn up and  i open my laptop it  it's actually remarkably difficult right  as we've seen you began the whole the  whole debate by saying  we've had sanctions for years in some of  these places and they're doing very well  but as as the the questioners  correctly imply with the  example they give about the illicit  trade in gold and  petroleum what happens  when you are a sanctioned country is you  kind of join this club  a pretty unappealing club with russia  and iran and all the rest of it  but they kind of close ranks and they  create what tom  burgess just ably called a a big global  black market that is operating outside  all of the normal methods and laws  of global trade and so the one thing we  know about operating in that black black  market  is that it doesn't benefit any ordinary  people because it's a  an illicit kind of cartel  it's just like the drugs trade you are  gonna you're gonna create fortunes  for a few connected individuals  cronies of the regime and their families  but you're not gonna have  any benefit for the wider population and  that in a nutshell  is why sanctions are worse than useless  free trade is a way of lifting ordinary  people  ending poverty spreading the benefits  sanctions or any other trade  restrictions  tend to funnel the benefits and hurt the  wrong people  do you agree with dan and that sanctions  he's obviously against the generalized  ones but he seems also to be against  most of the targeted ones he says they  could only really work at the margins  do you agree with that well i think  there's certainly a role for some  sanctions as i say in protecting  ourselves i mean  the flow of dirty money through the uk  whether that's  uh you know buying property or as you  raised it  paying for educational various other  things can corrupt  our own systems can undermine uh our our  own democracy and can  weaken our own defense so i think  there's certainly an element of  sanctions  there but i'd see that more as national  security related  but in terms of actually changing state  policy i mean it's quite clear that you  know sanctioning one of the richest men  in china chairman xi  he's not going to change chinese state  policy significantly nor would  sanctioning president putin in russia  and so there i think that uh he's got a  very  good point which is you know as dan says  you know handing control of an economic  of economic levers  to fewer and fewer people is guaranteed  to result in one thing which is the  greater enrichment of those few  and it will look even if it's not the  reality it will look  as though those sanctioning are simply  doing it in order to  empower those few and so it's  it's certainly something that one should  be extremely cautious of  actually i'd add one category where i  think  sanctions are valid and that is uh for  example in the case of china if you have  a prohibition  on slave-made produce  that it seems to me is a a legitimate  and  proportionate way of uh  uh of trying to reduce a barbarity in  another country  maya lester is it  the function of sanctions i mean tom  took it hat is mentioning  two things really there about sanctions  what one  is as a tool of foreign policy and the  second is  to try and clean up the laundromat  reputation of london and its own viral  do we have evidence that sanctions have  worked in either case  now i think as i said in my opening  remarks it's really dangerous to  conflate sanctions  with law enforcement or even with  national security  so the problem that another a number of  panelists have rightly identified is the  problem of dirty money as it were being  able to circulate too freely  in london and you made the point that  you know  is actually fighting financial crime in  london  a priority area or do we actually quite  like the funds in london i think this is  a really serious issue because  serious fraud is not very actively  prosecuted in the united kingdom  and obviously should be is there we ask  ourselves a lack of government desire to  do so is it a problem of  underfunding as tom burgess suggests  interesting problem but i think it's  certainly a problem however  what is very problematic is using  sanctions where we should be using law  enforcement now clearly  this is for only for cases where we can  use law enforcement  and that's basically where there is a uk  connection so we're talking about the  problem in the united kingdom  the problem it seems to me with using  sanctions by which we mean  basically asset freezes and travel bans  there are several problems first of all  the standards drop immediately we're not  talking any more about criminal trials  with proper standards  standards of evidence we're talking  about a much lower threshold which  creates serious problems where we're  setting an example to the rest of the  world but the other problem is where  does it end if we start putting people  on sanctions lists these are non-state  actors because they've done something  wrong  and that is opened up beyond the uk  we're going to be in the business where  we have a never-ending  you know people that we regard as bad  being sanctioned by the united kingdom  on the basis of very low thresholds of  evidence and that i think is not a place  we want to get to i want to just make a  couple of very brief  points first of all i didn't wish to  suggest  that sanctions don't ever work the main  point i want to make is that we don't  analyze whether they work in advance  very effectively and we don't have a  clear strategy for determining  how it is that they're supposed to work  but i do think it's significant  and we know this that president putin is  bothered by sanctions  we also know that china is bothered by  sanctions which is exactly why we have  the statement  that tom burgess referred to where you  know we get counter sanctions but also  will should we start getting less  dependent on the dollar  now it seems to me that you know when  people are bashing president trump's  uh door down to try to get sanctions  lifted that must be because in some  sense they are hurting  and i think you know sanctions on russia  much maligned  there is to some extent of strategy  which is if we place sufficient pressure  on the state-owned banks and financial  institutions  that will cost the state dear but so  what we don't have is a very clear  analysis  of how are they supposed to work and are  they working  and indeed as today has clearly  highlighted the relationship with trade  policy and that also needs studying  and just finally i also don't want to  give the impression that we're in a due  process black hole  so it's definitely not the case that  people are just added to lists with no  due process at all  that used to be the case and post 911  alleged terrorists were rounded up and  put on  um financial sanctions lists with  literally no reasons given now  the uk's new sanctions legal framework  does have a process for judicial  challenge for evidence being released  and so on  but it's a process which is appropriate  to the sort of foreign policy framework  and not i think one that's appropriate  given  the very very significant impact that it  can have  on those who are subject to sanctions  finally just a quick correction dan  gertler still is subject to u.s  sanctions what happened was he had a  license granted him by the u.s  authorities for a year to be able to  do certain things with his funds and it  was that license that was revoked  but he is still very much subject to  sanctions in the united states  tom i see you want to come back there  tom well but just on that point i mean  that's why i said he  the sanctions were effectively relaxed  yeah i agree that he is still  technically subject to magnitsky  sanctions but he only needed a license  for one day to move all his money out of  the u.s  and the biden administration i believe  has now reim  revoked that license but um  in in real terms what happened was  through um clandestine  uh lobbying efforts gertler  was freed from the effect of those  sanctions for a  period of time and that the big worry  was that was  long enough for him to do whatever he  did with any  assets that may have been frozen well so  this again highlights just  another problem i want to reiterate i  won't say anything about the specifics  of  of of that case but what i will say  is you contrast the case of someone  who's added to a sanctions list  with someone who's subject to a criminal  process and prosecution  and the truth is if someone is added to  a sanctions list  and they strongly deny what is said  against them  uh then um you know they can be added to  a sanctions list  and journalists and anyone around the  world uh and the public at large will  think that what is said about them is  correct it has an incredibly  far-reaching effect on reputation  and my point is that is not something  that should be encouraged  where when you're making allegations as  serious as grand corruption  somebody is normally entitled to full  due process of law  all right we're coming to the end now of  our discussion on  uh sanctions uh just two comments here  one from james arnold he  it says sanctioned states find  themselves members of the sanctions club  effectively means that you build a  coalition against the west  and another point which resonates too  from wonky hills saying isn't the  underlying context for this debate the  shift in the balance  of power away from western parts now  this brings me  to the final point which i'll get you  all to comment on something  tom burgess said very on resonated with  me a lot  because he said we should see this in  the context of an undeclared and  unconventional war  with authoritarianism and for me  the history of the 21st century so far  which was meant to be the century  that saw the ultimate triumph of  democracy the first two decades have  been  the consolidation and indeed expansion  of  authoritarianism certainly the growing  confidence of authoritarianism  and of course it is authoritarian  regimes to which we take sanctions  he's absolutely right on the fact that  there is a rise in authoritarianism and  for the first time  there is very little correlation or  sorry there is a decreasing correlation  between  uh rule of law and wealth there was a  very interesting article written by  james canagasorium it was published in  medium a number of months ago  that highlighted the trend going the  other way and the real question i think  therefore for all of us has to be  where do democracies stand and what does  it mean to stand up for the rule of law  and this is exactly where some of us  have been talking about looking at gat  in 1947 and remembering that it wasn't  actually initially set up as a trade  deal it was actually  a deal to resist soviet imperialism  that used trade as a leverage and today  we need to think hard about  how do we use trade and this dan's  spoken about very powerfully already and  maya has spoken tom has spoken  differently about is using  trade as a liberator not  just as an as a an enricher and  using it to defend the values that we  think matter  the freedoms of association of  expression the liberties that we think  are important to us as citizens and  therefore using it  to actually push back on autocracies  because instead what we've done is we've  opened up trade to the autocracies  demonstrating  what perhaps our uh ancestors knew in  1947 48  was that if you trade with the soviet  common turn you end up  importing their values not just their  goods  and what really worries me about this is  that  i mean andrew of course is is right  there has been a shift  to altogether more authoritarian regimes  china is the only major economy that is  bigger now than it was  a year ago uh weirdly you would think if  you were a visitor from another planet  that in terms of the texture of daily  life that russia and china were much  more sort of liberal places than  than britain you know in terms of the  the daily restrictions and what really  worries me  is the readiness as we come out of the  lockdown  for us to abandon our traditional  freedoms  here a little bit like after as you say  after 1945  we found that powers that we'd  surrendered to the state  on a supposedly contingent basis for the  purpose of the war  were then not returned when the peace  came you know we had identity cards  until 1952 we had food rationing until  1954. we had full conscription until  1960 and when it came to the economics  we really had  the command economy in place in some  areas until the thatcher reforms indeed  in some aspects of healthcare and  education we still have bits of it  today and my worry is that coming out of  the  uh epidemic and the lockdowns we will  see  a a similar demand for the smack of firm  government that people are not just  acquiescing  in the loss of their liberties they're  demanding it and and that to me  is the biggest authoritarian danger of  all it's not just a geopolitical one  it's an internal one happening within  our countries and indeed  within ourselves but that's what hostile  state agents are pushing  here too we do know and this is where  myers point about  dirty money and the use of law is of  course entirely valid but not  totally valid because actually some of  the sanctions are genuinely sanctions  in terms of national security we know of  individuals taking  money in order to undermine us directly  in the same ways some would plant a bomb  some plant disinformation and some use  corruption  and we've always come to the end about  time but i'd just like a final word from  maya then from tom burgess maya you  first  just on on your interesting question  about authoritarian regimes two comments  one i think the point about needing  strategy and needing to study likely  impacts  it it goes even more strongly where  we're talking about trying to impose  sanctions on authoritarian regimes  because of course  the sanctions premise particularly of  targeted sanctions  is that if you impose enough pressure on  people  they will rise up and they will you know  elect a different leader or they will  start pressuring  their leaders to change policy now that  strategy needs very careful  consideration  when we're not dealing with democratic  regimes so i absolutely agree  but second and final point is do we not  think  that yes there is a rise of  authoritarian regimes  but perhaps um listening to dan hannon's  point about  the trade bill and the discussions in  parliament and indeed among the general  public  that businesses and consumers are far  more aware  of the serious abuses of human rights by  those authoritarian regimes  and might that not present an  opportunity to explore other tools  than sanctions uh which is perhaps a  debate for another evening  all right thank you for that final quick  word please from you tom  tom burgess very quickly i just say that  maybe  we should remember that one man's free  trade is another man's um  energetic embrace of dictatorship um  look at the countries we've been talking  about tonight  iran saudi arabia venezuela russia uae  what they have in common they're all  petrol states  we we um we complain about their  corruption but we  by which i mean in each of those cases  western  multinationals in whom all our pensions  are invested  pump their oil and do business with  their dictatorships and it's it's  it's actually the petro economy that  creates the worst  kleptocracies and until we turn off our  addiction to  oil that's not going to change  right thank you for that tom we'll leave  it there tom duggan  uh dan hannah maya lester tom burgess  thank you  all for being such a splendid panel  tonight on our discussion  on sanctions thanks to the initiative  for free trade for sponsoring this event  and making it possible and of course  thanks to the audience many of you out  there for watching and for sending in  your questions you can re-watch and  share  today's discussion because it's going to  be posted  on spectator tv's youtube  channel so that's spectator tv on  youtube  and you can watch it go back over the  points share it with your friends but  for all  of you watching for all of you  participating that's the end of our show  for spectator tv  for now thank you for watching goodbye
 

